1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,155
Let's start with the simplest object
in linear algebra, the vector.

2
00:00:04,155 --> 00:00:08,744
Picture an arrow that begins at the
origin of a grid and points somewhere.

3
00:00:08,744 --> 00:00:11,287
Only its length and its direction matter.

4
00:00:11,287 --> 00:00:14,140
Think of it as an
instruction for how to move.

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:15,752
We will call this arrow v.

6
00:00:16,102 --> 00:00:19,719
To describe v with numbers,
read off where its tip lands.

7
00:00:19,719 --> 00:00:23,081
Walk three units along the
x axis, then two units up.

8
00:00:23,081 --> 00:00:28,157
Those two numbers, three and two, stacked
in a column, are the coordinates of v.

9
00:00:28,157 --> 00:00:33,296
Every arrow on this grid matches exactly
one such pair, so the list and the arrow

10
00:00:33,296 --> 00:00:34,502
are the same thing.

11
00:00:34,852 --> 00:00:37,040
Adding vectors has a lovely picture.

12
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:41,354
Take a second vector, w, and slide
it so its tail sits on the tip of v.

13
00:00:41,354 --> 00:00:44,878
The arrow from the origin to
where you end up is v plus w.

14
00:00:44,878 --> 00:00:48,160
With numbers, you simply add
the matching coordinates:

15
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:52,292
three plus one is four, and two plus
negative three is negative one.

16
00:00:52,642 --> 00:00:56,535
Multiplying by a number, called
a scalar, stretches the arrow.

17
00:00:56,535 --> 00:01:01,808
Two times v points the same way but is twice
as long, and negative one times v flips

18
00:01:01,808 --> 00:01:02,436
it around.

19
00:01:02,436 --> 00:01:04,508
Adding arrows and scaling arrows:

20
00:01:04,508 --> 00:01:07,082
that is the whole game of linear algebra.
